---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def, { doseText } from '../calculators/pool-chemical';
import { CHLORINE_PRODUCTS, chlorineDose, bakingSodaPounds, stabilizerPounds, calciumPounds, fcTargets, CALCIUM_PRODUCTS } from '../lib/pool-chem';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';

const sizes = [10000, 15000, 20000, 25000];
const shown = ['liquid10', 'liquid125', 'bleach825', 'calhypo65', 'dichlor'].map((id) => CHLORINE_PRODUCTS.find((p) => p.id === id)!);
const cyas = [0, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80].map((c) => ({ c, ...fcTargets(c) }));
const dry = (lb: number) => doseText({ pounds: lb, ounces: lb * 16, gallons: 0, liquid: false });
---

<CalcPage
  slug="pool-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Pool Calculator: Chlorine, Shock, Alkalinity & Stabilizer Doses"
  description="Free pool chemical calculator. Enter your gallons and test results to get how much liquid chlorine, shock, baking soda, stabilizer, or calcium chloride to add."
  h1="Pool Chemical Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Work out how much chlorine, shock, baking soda, stabilizer, or calcium chloride to add, from your pool's gallons and
    your latest test results. Every dose shows the math behind it.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to use the pool calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Know your pool's volume in gallons. If you don't, the <a href="/pool-volume-calculator/">pool volume calculator</a> works it out from the shape.</li>
    <li>Test the water with a drop-based kit or a reliable strip, and note the current reading.</li>
    <li>Pick what to adjust, enter the current and target levels, and choose the product you have.</li>
    <li>Add one chemical at a time with the pump running, then retest before adding anything else.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="chlorine">How much chlorine to add</h2>
  <p>
    To raise free chlorine by 1 ppm in 10,000 gallons you need about <strong>12.8 fl oz of 10% liquid chlorine</strong>,
    10.2 fl oz of 12.5%, or about 2 oz of 65% cal-hypo. Scale by your gallons and by how many ppm you want to add.
  </p>
  <p>Amount to raise free chlorine by 1 ppm:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Product</th>{sizes.map((g) => <th class="num">{(g / 1000).toFixed(0)}k gal</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{shown.map((p) => <tr><td>{p.label}</td>{sizes.map((g) => <td class="num">{doseText(chlorineDose(p, g, 1))}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="fc-cya">How much chlorine does my pool need? It depends on stabilizer</h2>
  <p>
    Cyanuric acid (CYA, stabilizer) protects chlorine from sunlight but also makes it weaker, so the right chlorine level
    rises with CYA. The widely used Trouble Free Pool guideline keeps free chlorine at about 7.5% of CYA as a minimum,
    and raises it to about 40% of CYA to shock (SLAM) an algae problem.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>CYA</th><th class="num">Minimum FC</th><th class="num">Target FC</th><th class="num">Shock FC</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{cyas.slice(1).map((r) => <tr><td>{r.c} ppm</td><td class="num">{fmt(r.minimum, 1)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(r.target, 1)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(r.shock, 1)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="shock">How much shock do I need?</h2>
  <p>
    Many shock bags say one pound treats 10,000 gallons, which with 65% cal-hypo raises chlorine by roughly 8 ppm. Whether
    that is enough depends on your stabilizer: at 40 ppm CYA a real algae shock needs about 16 ppm. Enter your CYA and the
    calculator shows the shock level and the dose to reach it. Liquid chlorine shocks without adding calcium or CYA, which
    is why many pool owners prefer it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="alkalinity">How much baking soda to raise alkalinity</h2>
  <p>
    About <strong>1.4 lb of baking soda per 10,000 gallons raises total alkalinity by 10 ppm</strong>. For a 15,000-gallon
    pool going from 60 to 90 ppm, that is {dry(bakingSodaPounds(15000, 30))}.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>To raise by</th>{sizes.map((g) => <th class="num">{(g / 1000).toFixed(0)}k gal</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{[10, 20, 30].map((d) => <tr><td>{d} ppm TA</td>{sizes.map((g) => <td class="num">{dry(bakingSodaPounds(g, d))}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="stabilizer">How much stabilizer (CYA) to add</h2>
  <p>
    About 13 oz of cyanuric acid raises 10,000 gallons by 10 ppm. It dissolves slowly, so wait several days before
    retesting, and add it gradually: the only way to lower CYA is to replace water. Salt pools usually run 60–80 ppm,
    other outdoor pools 30–50 ppm.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>To raise by</th>{sizes.map((g) => <th class="num">{(g / 1000).toFixed(0)}k gal</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{[10, 20, 30].map((d) => <tr><td>{d} ppm CYA</td>{sizes.map((g) => <td class="num">{dry(stabilizerPounds(g, d))}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="calcium">How much calcium chloride to add</h2>
  <p>
    To raise calcium hardness by 10 ppm in 10,000 gallons, add about {dry(calciumPounds(10000, 10, CALCIUM_PRODUCTS[0].purity))}
    of 94–97% calcium chloride, or {dry(calciumPounds(10000, 10, CALCIUM_PRODUCTS[1].purity))} of 77–80% flakes. Plaster
    pools are commonly kept around 250–350 ppm; vinyl and fiberglass pools can run lower.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ph">Why the calculator doesn't dose pH</h2>
  <p>
    How far a cup of acid moves pH depends on alkalinity and other chemistry, so a single formula can easily overshoot.
    The safer method: with the pump running, pour a small measured dose of muriatic acid slowly in front of a return jet,
    for example 8 fl oz of 31.45% acid per 10,000 gallons, wait 30 minutes, retest, and repeat. Always add acid to water,
    never water to acid, and wear gloves and eye protection. Most pools are kept between pH 7.2 and 7.8.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Can I add chlorine and other chemicals at the same time?</h3>
  <p>
    Add them one at a time, well spread out, with the pump running. Never mix chemicals in a bucket or feeder; cal-hypo
    and trichlor in particular can react violently if they come into contact.
  </p>
  <h3>Why does my chlorine keep dropping?</h3>
  <p>
    Sun, swimmers, and organic debris use it up. If it drops more than about 1 ppm overnight with the pool covered or the
    sun down, something is still consuming it, often early algae, and a shock is worth considering.
  </p>
  <h3>What about salt pools?</h3>
  <p>A salt system makes its own chlorine. Use the <a href="/salt-pool-calculator/">salt pool calculator</a> to keep the salt level where the cell needs it.</p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Doses are linear: gallons × 8.345 lb × 1e-6 × ppm change, divided by the product's strength.</li>
    <li>Baking soda uses 1.68 lb per lb of alkalinity (as CaCO₃); calcium chloride 1.11, adjusted for purity.</li>
    <li>Liquid chlorine strengths are pool-trade percentages; bleach labels are by weight, which the calculator converts.</li>
    <li>FC targets follow the Trouble Free Pool FC/CYA guideline. Always follow the product label and retest after every dose.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
